            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"Great Expectations\" and \"Bleak House\"</strong><br>\nGreat Expectations and \"Bleak House\" are considered some of Dickens's greatest literary achievements. They exhibit his skill at creating complex plots and tackling issues like social reform and the justice system.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Dickens's Journalistic Ventures</strong><br>\nBesides novels, Dickens also edited a weekly journal, \"Household Words,\" and later \"All the Year Round,\" which featured serialized novels, including his own, and focused on social reform.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nDickens died in 1870, leaving \"The Mystery of Edwin Drood\" unfinished. He is buried in the Poets' Corner of Westminster Abbey. His work continues to be popular and influential, reflecting his belief in social reform and the innate goodness of people.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
